{
  // Output filename and BoardLoader data-variable name:
  //   boardName "RP2040" -> designer/boards/RP2040/RP2040.json -> const RP2040Data = {...}
  "boardName": "RP2040",

  // Fallback human-readable name shown in the designer UI.  Overridden
  // by the matching `<id>.name=` entry in a sibling-or-ancestor
  // boards.txt when one is present (matched via `<id>.build.variant=`
  // against this variant's directory name) — for this board that's
  // "iLabs Connectivity 2040 LTE/WiFi/BLE" (variants/rp2040/boards.txt).
  "displayName": "Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040)",

  // Same parser dialect as every other RP2040 board — see
  // ../board.json for the full rationale.
  "family": "rp2040",

  "inputOnlyGpios": [],

  // Default transports — Serial only. This board's onboard WiFi/BLE
  // co-processor is an ESP32-C3, reached over a plain UART (Serial1 /
  // GPIO16-17) plus GPIO24/25 reset/boot-select control lines, driven
  // via the WiFiEspAT AT-command library (confirmed by this board's own
  // boards.txt entry: `connectivity_2040_lte_wifi_ble.build.extra_flags=
  // -DWIFIESPAT2`) — NOT the native RP2040 + CYW43439 combo that Pico W /
  // rp2040w-bucket boards use. Its onboard cellular modem (u-blox
  // SARA-R412M, LTE-M/NB-IoT/eGPRS) is likewise reached over a plain UART
  // (Serial2 / GPIO4-7) plus GPIO12-15 control lines, not an IP-capable
  // native stack either. variants/rp2040/tcp.ino, http.ino and ble.ino are
  // written against the native cyw43 WiFi.h stack, so they do not apply
  // here — this board would need its own dedicated WiFiEspAT/AT-command
  // modem .ino templates before it could genuinely offer tcp/http/ble
  // connections. Plain "rp2040" (Serial only) is correct, matching the
  // same reasoning already established for the challenger_2040_wifi
  // (ESP8285 AT co-processor) and challenger_2040_lte (SARA-R4XX modem)
  // siblings audited earlier this session.
  //
  // pfodWeb NOTE (WiFi-bucketing flag, 2026-07-13 audit): this board DOES
  // have real onboard WiFi/BLE silicon (the ESP32-C3 co-processor,
  // confirmed via iLabs' own datasheet) plus real onboard cellular
  // silicon (SARA-R412M), unlike most other boards correctly left in the
  // plain "rp2040" bucket for lacking any such hardware. It sits here
  // deliberately because the mechanism differs from the native cyw43
  // stack (UART-attached AT-command co-processor/modem vs. SPI-attached
  // native radio) rather than through oversight. Flagging per this
  // audit's standing instruction to surface (not silently fix) any
  // WiFi-bucketing question — no chip-bucket move made in this task.
  "connections": ["serial"],

  "pinNotes": {},

  // pfodWeb NOTE (dedicated-hardware pin-exclusion + LED/NeoPixel tagging
  // audit, 2026-07-13):
  //
  // Board: iLabs "RP2040 Connectivity" board (marketed/boards.txt name
  // "Connectivity 2040 LTE/WiFi/BLE"), combining an RP2040 with an
  // onboard u-blox SARA-R412M LTE-M/NB-IoT/eGPRS cellular modem AND an
  // onboard ESP32-C3 WiFi/BLE co-processor on one 55mm x 42mm board.
  //
  // Sources consulted:
  // 1. https://ilabs.se/connectivity-rp2040-lte-wifi-ble-datasheet/
  //    (official iLabs datasheet, fetched directly) — confirms verbatim:
  //    "GPIO19 (D19/LED_BUILTIN) is connected to the user LED. Setting
  //    this pin high will light the on board green LED up." (active-high,
  //    stated explicitly, not inferred); "GPIO16 (D16): TXD to ESP32
  //    RXD", "GPIO17 (D17): RXD from ESP32 TXD", "GPIO24 (D24): ESP32
  //    reset input", "GPIO25 (D25): ESP32 boot select pin (active-low for
  //    programming mode)" (WiFi/BLE co-processor link); "GPIO4 (D4):
  //    UART1 TXD", "GPIO5 (D5): UART1 RXD", "GPIO6 (D6): UART1 CTS",
  //    "GPIO7 (D7): UART1 RTS", "GPIO12 (D12): DTR signal", "GPIO13
  //    (D13): Power-on signal", "GPIO14 (D14): Reset signal", "GPIO15
  //    (D15): Power supply control" (SARA-R412M modem link). No
  //    NeoPixel/addressable RGB LED anywhere on the board. No dedicated
  //    user pushbutton beyond the fixed-function reset and BOOT (BOOTSEL)
  //    buttons.
  // 2. https://www.cnx-software.com/2024/03/15/rp2040-connectivity-board-iot-solution-combining-lte-wi-fi-and-ble-into-a-single-platform/
  //    and https://www.electronics-lab.com/ilabs-new-rp2040-connectivity-board-features-u-blox-sara-r412m-lte-cat-m1-nb-iot-and-esp32-modules/
  //    (independent press coverage, via WebSearch) — corroborate: RP2040
  //    + u-blox SARA-R412M (LTE Cat M1/NB-IoT) + ESP32-C3FN4 (WiFi/BLE),
  //    vendor iLabs, no mention of any onboard NeoPixel or extra button.
  // 3. This board's own pins_arduino.h
  //    (variants/rp2040/rp2040/connectivity_2040_lte_wifi_ble/pins_arduino.h)
  //    — already carries a prior-session "pfodWeb NOTE" (lines 54-57)
  //    recording that GPIO4,5,16,17,24,25 (ESP32 link) and GPIO6,7,12,
  //    13,14,15 (SARA-R4XX modem link) are deliberately excluded from the
  //    `static const uint8_t Dn` alias list. Verified directly: the only
  //    Dn aliases present are D0-D3, D8-D11, D18-D23, D26-D29 — none of
  //    {4,5,6,7,12,13,14,15,16,17,24,25} appear anywhere. This already
  //    fully excludes all twelve co-processor/modem-dedicated GPIOs from
  //    the Designer's pin list with zero board.json involvement. GPIO19
  //    (the LED) DOES carry a plain D19 alias, so it needs a chipGpios
  //    entry to be tagged as an LED rather than left as a bare GPIO.
  // 4. pfodWeb_src/build_boards.js (read directly, not executed) —
  //    confirms the same allGpios/inPinsH mechanism already traced and
  //    documented on the challenger_2040_lte sibling's board.json: a
  //    chipGpios entry for a GPIO that is ALSO absent from pins_arduino.h
  //    would needlessly re-add it as a bare "GPIO<n>" row carrying an
  //    auto-appended "Check if available on board" note. Since GPIO4,5,
  //    6,7,12,13,14,15,16,17,24,25 already lack pins_arduino.h aliases
  //    (source #3 above), no chipGpios entries are added for them here —
  //    matching the challenger_2040_lte precedent's carefully-traced
  //    reasoning (not the earlier, inconsistent challenger_2040_wifi/nfc
  //    audits that added redundant entries for already-unaliased pins).
  //
  // Full findings/sources trail:
  // boardsDetails/rp2040/rp2040/connectivity_2040_lte_wifi_ble/notes.txt
  //
  // Per-pin reasoning:
  //   GPIO4  - UART1 TXD - SARA-R412M modem's dedicated UART TX line.
  //            Not general-purpose. Already absent from pins_arduino.h
  //            aliases; no chipGpios entry added (see above).
  //   GPIO5  - UART1 RXD - SARA-R412M modem's dedicated UART RX line.
  //            Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO6  - UART1 CTS - SARA-R412M modem's hardware flow-control CTS
  //            line. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO7  - UART1 RTS - SARA-R412M modem's hardware flow-control RTS
  //            line. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO12 - DTR signal - SARA-R412M modem's DTR control line, driven
  //            by the RP2040. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO13 - Power-on signal - SARA-R412M modem's power-on control
  //            line, driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO14 - Reset signal - SARA-R412M modem's reset control line,
  //            driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO15 - Power supply control - SARA-R412M modem's power-supply
  //            LDO enable line, driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as
  //            GPIO4.
  //   GPIO16 - TXD to ESP32 RXD - ESP32-C3 WiFi/BLE co-processor's
  //            dedicated UART link (RP2040 TX side). Not general-purpose.
  //            Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO17 - RXD from ESP32 TXD - ESP32-C3 co-processor's dedicated
  //            UART link (RP2040 RX side). Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO24 - ESP32 reset input - ESP32-C3 co-processor's reset control
  //            line, driven by the RP2040. Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO25 - ESP32 boot select pin - ESP32-C3 co-processor's active-low
  //            boot/programming-mode select line, driven by the RP2040.
  //            Same reasoning as GPIO4.
  //   GPIO19 - PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN (D19 alias) - onboard general-
  //            purpose user LED. Confirmed active-high directly by the
  //            official iLabs datasheet's own wording ("Setting this pin
  //            high will light the on board green LED up") — not an
  //            inference. Tagged led_high; digital_input dropped to match
  //            this repo's established precedent for dedicated onboard
  //            LED pins; pwm_output kept since arduino-pico's
  //            analogWrite() works on any RP2040 GPIO, enabling
  //            brightness dimming.
  //
  // GPIO19 carries no SPI/I2C/other bus alias on this board (SPI0 lives
  // on GPIO20-23, Wire0 on GPIO0-1 — confirmed by grepping the whole
  // pins_arduino.h for SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK/SDA/SCL), so the build_boards.js
  // unconditional bus-capability auto-append (the "confirmed button/LED
  // pin also aliased as SDA/SCL/SS/MOSI/MISO/SCK" bug class flagged for
  // this audit) does not apply here — no pins_arduino.h edit was needed.
  // Likewise, none of the twelve dedicated co-processor/modem GPIOs
  // coincide with the SPI0/Wire0 pins either.
  //
  // Also confirmed: no onboard NeoPixel and no onboard user button
  // (beyond the standard RP2040 reset/BOOTSEL buttons, not normal GPIOs,
  // out of scope per the rpipico/board.json precedent) on this board —
  // neither the iLabs datasheet nor independent press coverage mention
  // either.
  "chipGpios": {
    "19": { "capabilities": ["digital_output", "pwm_output", "led_high"], "note": "PIN_LED / LED_BUILTIN (D19 alias) — onboard general-purpose user LED. Confirmed active-high directly by the official iLabs datasheet's own wording (\"Setting this pin high will light the on board green LED up\")." }
  },

  // Same 8-bit analogWrite() default as every other RP2040/RP2350 board.
  "pwm": { "min": 0, "max": 255, "method": "analogWrite" },

  // No hardware DAC on RP2040/RP2350.
  "dac": {},

  // RP2040's one 12-bit ADC (0-4095), ~3.3V reference — same as every
  // other RP2040 board. Matches this board's own local common.h
  // (NUM_ANALOG_INPUTS=4, ADC_RESOLUTION=12u).
  "adc": { "bits": 12, "max": 4095, "defaultRefVolts": "3.3" }
}
